==============================================================================
B.6 INTEGRITY - INVARIANTS
==============================================================================
INVARIANT checks - each must return 0. A FAIL is real inconsistent
data, not a cosmetic warning.

  alloc_le_donated                   PASS  n=0
      asserts: delivered + spoiled never exceeds the donated quantity of an item (correlated subqueries, so neither child table fans out)
  delivery_item_same_donation        PASS  n=0
      asserts: every delivery_item points at a food item of the delivery's donation
  moved_delivery_has_passed          PASS  n=0
      asserts: no IN_TRANSIT/DELIVERED delivery unless the LATEST inspection passed; no inspection at all counts as a failure
  route_driver_has_role_active       PASS  n=0
      asserts: every ACTIVE route's designated driver holds the DRIVER role now; inactive routes are excluded because their designation is historical after an approved role change
  branch_manager_has_role            PASS  n=0
      asserts: every branch's regional manager still has the NGO_MANAGER role
  route_assignment_role_allowed      PASS  n=0
      asserts: every route_assignment belongs to a DRIVER or SAFETY_INSPECTOR; NGO_MANAGER and DONOR are never assignable
  no_orphan_branch_refs              PASS  n=0
      asserts: app_user.primary_branch_id and branch.organization_id resolve

  Declarative constraints are compared in B.3 against the catalog.
  Positive data cannot prove enforcement: these checks would pass
  even if the server ignored every CHECK. B.10 tests the logic of
  the inspection gate; --negative-probes tests the server's CHECKs.

==============================================================================
B.7 INTEGRITY - OBSERVATIONS
==============================================================================
OBSERVATION checks - business signals, not defects.

  donations_without_items            n=0
      meaning: donation headers with no items
  deliveries_without_items           n=0
      meaning: deliveries with nothing allocated yet (normal for PLANNED)
  approved_without_inspection        n=0
      meaning: APPROVED/PARTIALLY_DISTRIBUTED/COMPLETED with no PASSED inspection - state drift the API state machine prevents going forward
  duplicate_branch_deliveries        n=0
      meaning: same donation to the same branch more than once - allowed by decision, surfaced so allocation can explain it
  expired_items_still_open           n=0
      meaning: items past best_before on PENDING/SCHEDULED/COLLECTED donations
  branches_without_manager           n=0
      meaning: branches with no regional manager (nobody can review deliveries)
  spoilage_by_non_operational        n=0
      meaning: spoilage reported by a role that cannot normally witness it
  review_before_handover             n=0
      meaning: reviews whose reviewer is no longer the receiving branch's manager - expected after a manager handover; the trigger guarantees the relationship at insert time, so this is a signal, not corruption
  delivery_driver_history            NOT AVAILABLE
      DELIVERY.driver_user_id is not present; the actual driver of a historical delivery cannot be established from the pre-F4 schema. DELIVERY stores only route_id, and ROUTE.designated_driver_user_id is the route's current designation, not necessarily the driver at delivery time. No attribution is inferred and no id is backfilled. Becomes auditable once F4 adds a nullable delivery.driver_user_id.
  route_driver_role_changed_inactive n=0
      meaning: INACTIVE routes whose former designated driver is no longer a DRIVER - expected after a P1-approved role change; keeps the route history readable without treating it as corruption. The same active/historical split applies to deliveries above.

  route_assignment by role
    DRIVER                5  allowed
    SAFETY_INSPECTOR      3  allowed
    permitted roles: DRIVER, SAFETY_INSPECTOR

  The same rule is asserted as an INVARIANT in B.6 (route_assignment_role_allowed); the table above exists so a disallowed role is visible in context, not only as a count.

